A||||13||ChadMan||GM introduces electric power steering!  (not a joke)||||1||
Z||000000||ChadMan||05-31-2001||08:28 AM||||From <A HREF="http://www.roadandtrack.com:GM" TARGET=_blank>www.roadandtrack.com:</A> GM to Introduce Electric Power Steering<P>"General Motors announced that it will begin installing 12-volt all-electric power steering systems on some of its models beginning this year. <P>The first model line to get the system will be the new Saturn Vue SUV that goes into production this fall. Electric power steering will also be used on the 2004 Chevrolet Malibu and Pontiac Grand Am.<P>GM says the system will boost fuel economy by 0.5 to 1.0 miles per gallon. Manufacturing and assembly costs are also affected since electric power steering uses fewer parts by elimination of the hydraulic pump, fluid and hoses. GM's Italian partner, Fiat, is already using the system on its Punto model sold in Europe. The system was developed by Delphi Automotive Systems."<P>If it took them 14 model years to get it right, maybe we 88 owners should be glad we don't have electric power steering after all.<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 05-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||05-31-2001||09:58 AM||||I wonder who will be the first to adapt it for thier Fiero??<P>I actually like my steering better than power.  Power steering would feel too loose and wouldn't give you the right amount of road feel.  If I was having trouble steering my little Fiero, I would definatly have to go beef up my arms 'cause it really isnt that hard.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||05-31-2001||11:49 AM||||I would like power steering as I do a lot of city driving and parking.  Also I want 18" rims in the future and I might as well kiss that idea goodbye without power steering....||||reg||1
Z||000003||WampusCat||05-31-2001||11:52 AM||||Did the electric power steering ever make it onto any of the '88's or was that just a prototype thing?||||reg||1
Z||000004||saleenfiero||05-31-2001||12:41 PM||||I HEARD (like that means anyhting) that the system did make it into a very few fieros, but they yanked it out before they were sold because the decision to axe the fiero was given and the parts availability was not there.  Personally am I alone when I say that I really don't see the need for power steering , its not that damn hard, if the MaclarenF1 doesn't have it, why should we?  Dammit, lack of power steering makes you a man!  It puts lead in your pencil!||||reg||1
Z||000005||grinthock||05-31-2001||12:58 PM||||Are you alone?  HELL NO!  I have driven lots of different vehicles from Honda's to SUV's, Firebirds, Camaro, (and a 360 Modena).  I like the Fiero steering the best, LOTS of road feel, nice and tight.  It's not THAT difficult to turn it, I mean if you are having trouble, don't be a WHIMP!  I am no body-builder by any means (packing at least a 12 can case of coke down there!)  and my upper arm stregnth sucks and I have no problems.  I will admit, with the smaller MOMO Wheel though it makes it more difficult...  <P>I don't like power steering... AT ALL... I don't feel I have control||||reg||1
Z||000006||KRMFiero||05-31-2001||01:09 PM||||I like the steering on the Fiero, except if im turning at under 5mph or at a  dead stop then its rather hard hehe<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||05-31-2001||03:53 PM||||Hydraulic power steering's days are numbered. Delphi and others have been working on this for awhile. I'm suprised they are saying they will use 12V. Most cars heading in that direction are slated for 36/42V electrical systems. Some may have 12/14v feeds for accesories and legacy parts. Allot cars are expected to use the HV systems by the end of the decade.<P>It's expected to nearly eliminate belt driven devices.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||grinthock||05-31-2001||05:01 PM||||Yep as hybrid systems come in that run higher voltage you will see the core Voltage of the vehicles rise.  I think you will see 48V myself, it's a very common motor speed.  Not to mention alot of electronic parts can be had rated for that voltage.||||reg||1
