Z||000011||Nashco||05-31-2001||07:45 PM||||I was under the impression that it uses an electronic power assist; basically, an electric motor gives you some help turning the wheel.  I figure it will either feel like a power steering car without the engine running (can be tough), or a regular rack and pinion without any help (lots of variable depending on gearing and suspension).<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000012||gabriel||06-01-2001||01:02 PM||||Has anyone been able to transplant any other car's power rack & pinion into the Fiero?<P>  I've heard some people thinking about that conversion, but has anyone DONE it??||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||06-01-2001||02:39 PM||||The new charging system is already standardized by the industry. Cars using the new system should show up for either the 2003 or 2004 model years. It will use a 36V battery with a 42V alternator. In most cases, 12V legacy devices will be fed from a tap part way down the battery or by a seperate 14v alternator feed and a second 12V battery. While we commonly ignore it, The alternator you are all used to is considered a 14V alternator not 12V. (This is explained better in some of the articles ffrom sources below.)<P><BR>There have been a number of articles published on the new systems. you can find some of them in these places.<P><A HREF="http://www.motorage.com/" TARGET=_blank>Motor Age</A><P><A HREF="http://www.motor.com" TARGET=_blank>Motor Magazine</A><P><A HREF="http://www.delphiauto.com/" TARGET=_blank>Delphi</A> <P>You'll have to dig around the sites/site archives to find the articles etc.<P>||||reg||1
